+Version 1.7.4 corrects how the session supervisor holds and ends a session's process tree on Windows (the client's review of 1.7.2, C2). In 1.7.2, when no job object could be made, the session ran anyway and its tree fell back to `taskkill /T`; when the session's process could not join the job, 1.7.2 resumed it anyway and fell back the same way. `taskkill /T` finds descendants through their parent's process id, so a child whose parent had already exited was out of its reach; and with the parent gone, 1.7.2 could not tell whether anything of the tree was left, yet it reported nothing left running and no problem. Now the supervisor fails closed on Windows. The job object is made with kill-on-close and without breakaway, both read back from the job; the session's process is created suspended, assigned to the job, confirmed a member by `IsProcessInJob` and counted alone in the job's accounting, and only then resumed, so it cannot run its own code, start a process or send anything outside the job. If the job cannot be made, or the process cannot be assigned, confirmed or resumed, nothing runs: the suspended process is terminated and reaped, the job is closed, and `run_session` returns an explicit refusal (`refused`: true, `status`: `refused`) that names the step. There is no fallback. Whether the tree has gone is read only from the job's accounting (from the process group on Linux and macOS, as before), never from the leading process's exit or a `taskkill` result. The cleanup is `verified`, `failed` or `unknown`, and an unknown stays unknown (`left_running` and `gone` are null, never false): the session's own evidence is then not taken, a recovery runs, and its manifest names the cleanup (`session_tree_cleanup`). `status` is `ok` only for a session that ended by itself with exit code 0 and its own complete evidence, and whose tree was verified gone. The 7 tests of `tests/test_review_of_172.py` use real processes, a handshake file and a stub on 127.0.0.1, and force each job-object failure at the kernel32 boundary only. They were first run on the frozen 1.7.2 code on Windows: the 5 `test_w_...` tests fail by their output (with no job object, or with a process that did not join it, the session ran, its child outlived the supervisor and connected to the stub afterwards; an unreadable count was reported as nothing left running and no problem), and the 2 control tests pass (Windows leaves a child running when its parent exits, and `taskkill /T` on the parent cannot reach it; a working job object ends such a child). On 1.7.4 all 7 pass, and a refused process is shown, just before its end, as it was created: one thread, suspended once, no time charged, and no resume asked for. (Its CPU time after its end is not evidence: Windows may charge one clock tick of user time to a process terminated without ever running.) On Linux and macOS the tree is still a process group; the 3 tests that are not Windows-only run there too. The first build of 1.7.4, never published, failed `mypy` on Linux and macOS with three `name-defined` errors: its Windows-only start used names defined only on Windows, and a type check run on Windows reads the code for Windows alone. This build puts that code under its platform check; what runs on each platform is unchanged. Nothing else changed: the steps, the response checks, the payloads, the limits, the T05 replay checks of 1.7.3 and the live refusal are as before.
